; The differential probe: one source set, built natively here as the golden and
; mirrored into js/jsoo and js/melange. See .ai/js_backends_design.md.
;
; Two libraries rather than one, and two entry points rather than a flag,
; because melange can only reach the pure half: walk_core needs jsont alone and
; core needs fmt alone, while lib/native brings Bigarray (no melange support)
; and Jsont_bytesrw. Recording that boundary here as [probes_pure] vs
; [probes_native] lets js/melange copy a clean subset instead of excluding files
; by hand -- and keeps a section from silently drifting to the wrong side.
;
; [wrapped false] matches the repo convention, so the mirrors refer to
; [Probe_walk_core] etc. unqualified exactly as this directory does.

(library
 (name probes_pure)
 (wrapped false)
 (modules probe_walk_core probe_core)
 (libraries walk_core core fmt jsont))

(library
 (name probes_native)
 (wrapped false)
 (modules probe_tensor_json probe_native)
 (libraries probes_pure native native_op_walk walk_core core))

; Separate stanzas with disjoint [modules]: two singular executables cannot both
; own a module, and putting both names in one plural stanza would build each
; entry against the other's module for no reason.

; [byte] alongside [exe] is a triage tool, not an artifact anyone ships. When
; the jsoo diff goes red, running native_probe.bc under ocamlrun says
; immediately which side is at fault: js_of_ocaml compiles the BYTECODE, so a
; difference that survives between .bc and .exe is ocamlopt's, not jsoo's.
; That is how the FMA contraction described in probe_native.ml was pinned down.

(executable
 (name native_probe)
 (modes exe byte)
 (modules native_probe)
 (libraries probes_pure probes_native))

(executable
 (name subset_probe)
 (modules subset_probe)
 (libraries probes_pure))
